+{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_234","type":"agent_message","text":"Clean circuit passes. Swapped circuits stall because when a swap creates a mismatch, downstream gates can't infer roles, and my `pending.size > 0` throw fires. The single-swap case `z01↔a01`: xor1_1 drives `a01` (gets role t01), and1_1 drives `z01` (gets role a01). Sum gate consumes wire `t01` — but NO gate drives `t01` now (xor1 drives a01, and1 drives z01). So wire `t01` never gets a role → sum gate's input t01 has no role → stall.\n\nThe mismatch resolution must handle this: when a wire name has no producer with the matching role, the swap is identifiable. Let me handle pending gates: for a stalled gate, look at which input has no role (unproduced wire name); that wire name and the role-carrying wire that SHOULD have that name form a swap. Let me implement the resolution pass properly.\n\nActually, the cleaner logic: after propagation stalls, for each pending gate, try to infer its role by treating its inputs' roles flexibly — if gate is XOR(t01-role-carrying-wire?...). Let me instead detect: the wire names mentioned in pending gates' inputs that have NO role yet are the \"stolen\" names. A stolen name N (e.g. t01) has no producer with role N; meanwhile some wire W carries role N (e.g. a01 carries t01's role... no, xor1 drives a01 and got role t01, so wire a01 carries role t01). So swap = (stolen name t01, wire carrying t01's role = a01)? No wait, xor1 drives a01 with role t01 means the SIGNAL t01 flows on wire a01. The stolen name is t01 (nothing drives it). The swap partner: t01 (name) ↔ a01 (the wire now carrying t01's role)? But expected answer is z01↔a01. Hmm — xor1 drives a01 (role t01 → so wire a01 is \"really t01\"), and1 drives z01 (role a01 → wire z01 is \"really a01\"). The z01 role belongs to sum gate which is stalled. \n\nSo the swap is between the names `a01` and `z01`: and1 (role a01) drives z01; sum (role z01) would drive... it's stalled but its intended out is z01, currently driven by and1. The wires whose names don't match their carried role: a01 carries t01-role (mismatch!), z01 carries a01-role (mismatch!). But t01 also mismatches (carries nothing / not driven). Expected answer: a01, z01 only.\n\nThe rule: a wire is swapped iff it's DRIVEN by a gate whose role differs from the wire's own pristine role. Pristine role of wire name N = role N (by naming convention: in pristine circuit wire t01 has role t01, wire z01 has role z01). Wire a01 is driven by xor1 (role t01) ≠ a01 → swapped ✓. Wire z01 driven by and1 (role a01) ≠ z01 → swapped ✓. Wire t01: driven by NO gate → its \"driver role\" is undefined. In pristine circuit, wire t01 exists and is driven by xor1. In the swapped circuit, does a wire NAMED t01 exist as some gate's out? Sum gate's out? No, sum drives z01... and1 drives z01, xor1 drives a01, prop drives b01, or drives c01. Is t01 any gate's out? xor1's out is a01... The pristine t01 wire name — some gate must output it (all wire names in the circuit are preserved, just reassigned). Sum gate's out: in pristine it's z01. After swap, sum's out is... the swap was z01↔a01: gate that had out z01 (sum) now has out a01?? No wait — original test swap ['z01','a01']: sum gate's out becomes a01? and and1's out becomes z01? Then xor1 still drives t01! Let me recheck.\n\nThe 4-bit test pairs: ['z01','a01'] means the gate that should output z01 (sum gate) instead outputs a01, and the gate that should output a01 (and1) outputs z01. So sum.out=a01, and1.out=z01, xor1.out=t01 (unchanged). Then: xor1 drives t01 (role t01 ✓). and1 drives z01 with role a01 → z01 mismatch ✓. sum gate consumes t01 (role t01) and c00 (role c00) → infers role z01, drives a01 → a01 mismatch ✓. No stall! Why did it stall?\n\nThe stall must be from a different pair interaction. Single swap z01↔a01 alone: does it stall? and1 drives z01 (role a01). sum consumes t01, c00 → role z01, drives a01 ✓. prop consumes t01, c00 → role b01, drives b01 ✓. OR consumes a01... wait OR's inputs are (a01, b01) wires. Wire a01 is driven by sum gate (role z01). So OR consumes wire a01 (role z01) and b01 (role b01) → (z01, b01) doesn't match OR template (needs a01, b01) → STALL.\n\nSo the OR stalls. Then my pending>0 throw fires. The fix: for the stalled OR, its inputs carry roles (z01, b01); expected (a01, b01). The input with wrong role is wire a01 (carries z01-role, should carry a01-role). So wire a01 is swapped; its swap partner is the wire carrying a01's role = z01 (driven by and1 with role a01). So swap = {a01, z01} ✓. \n\nSo the resolution: for each stalled gate, compare input roles with expected template; the input wire whose carried role ≠ expected role is swapped, paired with the wire that carries its expected role. Let me implement: after propagation, for pending gates, determine the gate's intended template rule from the roles that ARE consistent, find the mismatching input, flag it + the wire carrying the needed role. Then re-run propagation (now that roles are consistent) and repeat."}}
+{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_235","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cd /workspace && python3 - << 'PYEOF'\nsrc = open('src/index.ts').read()\n\nold = src[src.index(\\\"  // Assign roles by repeatedly firing gates\\\"):src.index(\\\"  if (pending.size > 0) {\\\\n    throw new Error('Malformed circuit: could not resolve all gate roles');\\\\n  }\\\\n  return [...result].sort();\\\")]\n\nnew = \\\"\\\"\\\"  // Assign roles by repeatedly firing gates whose inputs both have roles.\n  // When a swap stalls propagation (some gate's inputs no longer match the\n  // template rule they should), resolve the mismatch: the gate's intended\n  // rule is identified by its op and its consistent input; the input wire\n  // carrying the wrong role was swapped with the wire that carries the role\n  // the gate needs. Record the swap, repair the wire roles, and continue.\n  const outRole = new Map<Gate, string>();\n  const swapped = new Set<string>();\n  const pending = new Set<Gate>(gates);\n\n  const roleCarrier = new Map<string, string>(); // role -> wire carrying it\n  const setRole = (wire: string, r: string): void => {\n    wireRole.set(wire, r);\n    roleCarrier.set(r, wire);\n  };\n\n  let progressed = true;\n  while (pending.size > 0) {\n    progressed = false;\n    for (const gate of [...pending]) {\n      const r1 = wireRole.get(gate.in1);\n      const r2 = wireRole.get(gate.in2);\n      if (r1 === undefined || r2 === undefined) continue;\n      const inferred = inferRole(gate.op, r1, r2, numBits);\n      if (inferred === undefined) continue;\n      outRole.set(gate, inferred);\n      setRole(gate.out, inferred);\n      pending.delete(gate);\n      progressed = true;\n    }\n    if (progressed) continue;\n    if (pending.size === 0) break;\n\n    // Resolution pass: find a pending gate whose intended rule can be\n    // identified and whose mismatch is a single swapped input.\n    let resolvedAny = false;\n    for (const gate of [...pending]